{{!-- The Feed's tag row: All, then the publication's busiest subjects.

	{{> "feed/filter-bar"}}

THE PILLS ARE LINKS, NOT A FILTER. In the design source this row is a set of buttons driving a
client-side filter over the whole archive, which works there because the archive is 18 posts on
one page. Here the Feed is 104 posts over six pages, and a filter that hid rows would hide only
the rows on the page the reader happens to be on. Filtering by tag on Ghost is a Surface of its
own — the tag archive — so each pill is a link to it, and "All" is a link back to this Surface's
first page.

That changes which attribute marks the active pill. `aria-pressed` belongs to a button; a link
to the page you are on is `aria-current="page"`. The `tag-pill` utility colours from
`aria-pressed`, so assets/css/screen.css adds the link case beside it — one selector, in the
Ghost-only stylesheet, because the reference build has no links here to colour.

`{{page_url 1}}` rather than a hardcoded path: the Feed's URL is the buyer's, named in their
routes.yaml, and page_url resolves it from the request — so this pill points at page 1 of
whatever URL this Surface is mounted on, from any page of it.

THE COUNTS ARE THE PUBLICATION'S, NOT THE PAGE'S. The design source counts each tag within the
18 posts it lists; a pill here links to a Surface listing every post under that tag, so the
number on it is that tag's real total.

NO SORT CONTROL. The design draws a "Newest first / Oldest first" toggle beside the pills.
Ghost paginates a collection server-side and offers no reader-facing order: routes.yaml can fix
one order per route, and nothing in a template or a URL can change it per request. A toggle
that reversed the eighteen rows on the current page would say "oldest first" and show the
newest eighteen posts backwards. Recorded as a concession in DECISIONS.md.

`aria-current="true"`, not `"page"`. The All pill marks the active filter within a set, which is
what `true` means; `page` would claim the link points at the page the reader is on, and from
`/archive/page/3/` it points at page 1. The fill is the same either way — screen.css colours on
the attribute's presence, not its value.

`{{#if tags}}` and not the get's `{{else}}`: get calls `options.inverse` only for an invalid
resource or an API error (core/frontend/helpers/get.js), never for an empty result, so a
publication that files nothing under a public tag would otherwise get a rule and 36px of padding
around a lone "All" pill that links to the page it is already on. --}}
{{#get "tags" include="count.posts" order="count.posts desc" limit="7" as |tags|}}
	{{#if tags}}
		<section
			id="archive"
			aria-label="{{t "Filter by tag"}}"
			class="container pt-9">
			<div class="border-border flex flex-wrap items-center gap-5 border-b pb-4">
				<div class="strip flex min-w-0 flex-[1_1_420px] gap-[9px] p-0.5">
					<a
						href="{{page_url 1}}"
						aria-current="true"
						class="tag-pill font-meta tracking-meta rounded-pill flex-none border px-[15px] py-2 text-[12.5px] whitespace-nowrap uppercase no-underline">{{t "All"}}</a>

					{{#foreach tags}}
						<a
							href="{{url}}"
							class="tag-pill font-meta tracking-meta rounded-pill flex-none border px-[15px] py-2 text-[12.5px] whitespace-nowrap uppercase no-underline">{{name}}
							<span class="font-mono text-[11px] opacity-70">{{count.posts}}</span></a>
					{{/foreach}}
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>
	{{/if}}
{{/get}}
